              <!--  What Is Ipamorelin?  -->
              <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4 text-primary" style="font-family: var(--font-heading)">What Is Ipamorelin?</h2>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-3">Ipamorelin is a synthetic pentapeptide growth hormone secretagogue. It was developed as part of a research programme investigating compounds capable of stimulating growth hormone release while maintaining greater selectivity than earlier growth hormone-releasing peptides.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-3">The original published research identified Ipamorelin as a pentapeptide with the sequence: <strong class="text-primary">Aib-His-D-2-Nal-D-Phe-Lys-NH₂</strong></p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-4">Ipamorelin was shown in experimental models to stimulate growth hormone release through a GHRP-like receptor mechanism. The compound demonstrated activity in rat pituitary-cell assays as well as in animal models. Subsequent research has investigated the pharmacology and pharmacokinetics of Ipamorelin, including studies in healthy human volunteers.</p>
                <div class="flex flex-col gap-5">
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Peptide Structure</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Ipamorelin consists of five amino-acid residues and incorporates several structural features that distinguish it from naturally occurring peptides. The commonly reported sequence is Aib-His-D-2-Nal-D-Phe-Lys-NH₂. Ipamorelin acetate is also encountered in research and analytical literature. Researchers should consult the applicable product documentation for the precise form and analytical characteristics of the supplied material.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Growth Hormone Secretagogue Activity</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">The original pharmacological research demonstrated that Ipamorelin stimulated growth hormone release from rat pituitary cells and produced growth hormone responses in rat and swine models. This activity made Ipamorelin an important research compound for investigating the mechanisms controlling growth hormone secretion.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Receptor Activity</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Ipamorelin acts through a GHRP-like receptor mechanism associated with the growth hormone secretagogue receptor. This receptor forms part of the signalling system involved in growth hormone secretion. Investigating compounds that activate this receptor has helped researchers understand the pharmacology of growth hormone secretagogues and their relationship with endogenous hormonal signalling.</p>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </section>

              <!--  How Is Ipamorelin Investigated in Research?  -->
              <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4 text-primary" style="font-family: var(--font-heading)">How Is Ipamorelin Investigated in Research?</h2>
                <div class="flex flex-col gap-5">
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Growth Hormone Release</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">The principal area of Ipamorelin research is its effect on growth hormone secretion. The original studies demonstrated growth hormone release in cultured rat pituitary cells as well as in animal models. In rat pituitary-cell assays, Ipamorelin produced a strong growth hormone response, while experiments in rats and swine demonstrated growth hormone release following administration.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Growth Hormone Secretagogue Receptor</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Ipamorelin is used in research involving the growth hormone secretagogue receptor, sometimes referred to as GHS-R or the ghrelin receptor. This receptor forms part of the signalling system involved in growth hormone secretion. Investigating compounds that activate this receptor has helped researchers understand the pharmacology of growth hormone secretagogues and their relationship with endogenous hormonal signalling.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Selectivity Research</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">One of the characteristics that originally distinguished Ipamorelin from several earlier GHRPs was its reported selectivity for growth hormone release. In the original experimental work, researchers examined several pituitary hormones and reported that Ipamorelin did not significantly increase ACTH or cortisol in the same manner observed with GHRP-2 and GHRP-6. This selectivity was one of the principal reasons for the compound's continued scientific interest.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Pharmacokinetic Research</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Ipamorelin has also been investigated in human pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic research. A study involving healthy male volunteers examined different infusion rates and measured both Ipamorelin concentrations and growth hormone responses. The study characterised the relationship between circulating Ipamorelin concentrations and the resulting growth hormone response. This research provides useful pharmacological information about the compound but should not be interpreted as evidence establishing Ipamorelin as an approved therapeutic product.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Comparative Secretagogue Research</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-2">Ipamorelin has been compared experimentally with other growth hormone secretagogues, including GHRP-2 and GHRP-6. These comparisons have examined factors such as:</p>
                    <ul class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed list-disc list-inside flex flex-col gap-1">
                      <li>Growth hormone potency</li>
                      <li>Growth hormone response</li>
                      <li>Receptor activity</li>
                      <li>Hormonal selectivity</li>
                      <li>Pharmacokinetic behaviour</li>
                      <li>Structure-activity relationships</li>
                    </ul>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </section>

